<?php
/**
 * Command console switch  (beta.11)
 *
 * Copy this file to console.php and set allow_console to false (the line at
 * the bottom) to remove the admin panel's generic command console from this
 * installation. `setup.sh` / `setup.bat` offer it as menu item 9.
 *
 * ⚠ IF THIS FILE IS ABSENT, THE CONSOLE IS OFFERED. That is the opposite of
 * deploy.php, and it is deliberate — see WHY IT IS ON BY DEFAULT below. You
 * only need this file to turn the console OFF.
 *
 * ⚠⚠ THIS IS NOT A SECURITY CONTROL.
 *   Every command run from the console goes through exactly the same
 *   permission check as a direct POST to /management/. The console grants
 *   nothing: a signed-in user can already call every command it lists, by
 *   hand, with curl, on an install where this is off. What you remove by
 *   turning it off is DISCOVERABILITY AND REACH — your users no longer hold a
 *   generic runner for the whole command surface, which narrows what a future
 *   authorization bug could be driven through. That is worth having, and it is
 *   not a boundary. Do not treat it as one, and do not skip a permission
 *   review because it is off.
 *
 * WHAT IT GOVERNS
 *   The /admin/command page — the command index and the per-command form — and
 *   its entry in the panel's navigation. Nothing else. The Management API at
 *   /management/ is untouched: every command stays reachable to every caller
 *   whose role allows it, which is what keeps headless callers, scripts and
 *   the panel's own operation working. The command HISTORY tab on that same
 *   page also stays: it is a record of what ran, not a way to run anything.
 *
 * WHY IT IS ON BY DEFAULT
 *   deploy.php defaults to denied because deploying writes generated PHP
 *   outside the project's own storage — an install nobody configured must not
 *   do that. The console writes nothing and grants nothing, so the same
 *   reasoning does not apply to it: a fresh install that has never been
 *   configured should still have its developer tooling. Fail-closed is right
 *   for a capability that grants something and wrong for a view onto
 *   capabilities the caller already has.
 *
 * WHEN TO TURN IT OFF
 *   A shared or hosted QuickSite whose users author sites and have no business
 *   holding a generic command runner. The panel's purpose-built pages — the
 *   visual editor, the sitemap, the media library, builds, members — cover the
 *   work; the console is the escape hatch into the raw API, and it is a
 *   reasonable thing to withhold.
 *
 * WHAT A BROKEN FILE MEANS
 *   OFF. An unreadable file, a syntax error, a file that is not PHP, a wrong
 *   return type, a missing key, and any value that is not boolean `true` all
 *   leave the console disabled, and the reason is written to the PHP error log
 *   rather than taking the request down. That is not inconsistent with the
 *   default above: an ABSENT file means nobody ever configured this, while a
 *   file that is present but broken means somebody did — and the only reason
 *   to create it is to turn the console off. A typo resolving the permissive
 *   way would silently re-open what you closed.
 */

return [
    'allow_console' => true,
];
